identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "70a8f7526f69a86b8b2e165d85be18374cc9cd6830af982ad41ddca69349071b"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "442e8807297dc2494084b95676f504ca51d5b48601a0223f31f0024d65b046fe"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "70a8f7526f69a86b8b2e165d85be18374cc9cd6830af982ad41ddca69349071b"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "70a8f7526f69a86b8b2e165d85be18374cc9cd6830af982ad41ddca69349071b"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "70a8f7526f69a86b8b2e165d85be18374cc9cd6830af982ad41ddca69349071b"} {"question" "e-4411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4411.json" "file_sha256" "4272b005767be26669bcaf030ca3970beb7632f2176cf544ab0ed70202140102"} {"question" "e-4411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4411.json" "file_sha256" "4272b005767be26669bcaf030ca3970beb7632f2176cf544ab0ed70202140102"} {"question" "e-4412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4412.json" "file_sha256" "570a82dbe16295fb2f5d395185cba64f0011d454f11ea88f65dcfdae90d069cc"} {"question" "e-4412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4412.json" "file_sha256" "570a82dbe16295fb2f5d395185cba64f0011d454f11ea88f65dcfdae90d069cc"} {"question" "e-4413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\n- $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\n- $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4413.json" "file_sha256" "427e7d428a810cf7eb2e033e8e4e14b50a07cb6d6f607a52219d9438c336b57a"} {"question" "e-4413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4413.json" "file_sha256" "427e7d428a810cf7eb2e033e8e4e14b50a07cb6d6f607a52219d9438c336b57a"}] "usage" {"e-4413" [] "e-4412" [] "e-4411" []} "basis" 1054 "retractions" [[17592186114973 :question/problem "Find $AB-CB$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114973 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - C &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-I_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB - CB &= (A - C)B \\\\\n&=-I_{3}B \\\\\n&=-B \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114974 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186114974 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC)\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nABC &= A(BC) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114975 :question/problem "Given that $A, B$, and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n1. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n2. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n3. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$"] [17592186114975 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n$AB + AC \\neq BA + CA$.\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC$, and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
